- How would you extend to Unicode or larger alphabets while preserving performance?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design DP states that encode ordering constraints across variable-length tokens, a common real-world pattern in data parsing and tokenization.

## Key Concepts
- DP state design with (pos, prevLen) to compare adjacent tokens
- String comparison for equal-length numbers; no leading zeros handling
- Memoization vs bottom-up trade-offs for clarity and performance

## Code Example
```javascript
function countWays(s) {
  const n = s.length;
  const mod = 1_000_000_007;
  const memo = new Map();
  const dfs = (pos, prevLen) => {
    if (pos === n) return 1;
    const key = pos + ':' + prevLen;
    if (memo.has(key)) return memo.get(key);
    let res = 0;
    for (let nextLen = 1; nextLen <= n - pos; nextLen++) {
      if (s[pos] === '0') break;
      if (prevLen === 0 || nextLen > prevLen || (nextLen === prevLen && s.substring(pos, pos + nextLen) > s.substring(pos - prevLen, pos))) {
        res = (res + dfs(pos + nextLen, nextLen)) % mod;
      }
    }
    memo.set(key, res);
    return res;
  };
  return dfs(0, 0);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize equal-length comparisons to O(1) using precomputed LCP arrays?
- How would you adapt for non-strictly increasing sequences or allow leading zeros in some tokens?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of robust sorting strategies used in production libraries. Combines quicksort behavior, 3-way partitioning for duplicates, and a guaranteed worst-case fallback with heapsort. Assesses pivot choice, in-place constraints, and complexity reasoning.

## Key Concepts

- Introsort: hybrid of quicksort with depth-based fallback to heapsort
- 3-way partitioning: <, =, > pivot to handle duplicates efficiently
- Depth limit: d = floor(2*log2(n)) prevents O(n^2) degeneration
- In-place implementation with O(log n) recursion overhead

## Code Example

```javascript
function introsort(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  const depthLimit = n > 1 ? Math.floor(2 * Math.log2(n)) : 0;

  function swap(i, j) {
    const t = arr[i]; arr[i] = arr[j]; arr[j] = t;
  }

  function heapSortSub(start, end) {
    const len = end - start;
    function heapify(i, heapSize) {
      let largest = i;
      while (true) {
        const l = 2 * i + 1;
        const r = l + 1;
        let idx = i;
        if (l < heapSize && arr[start + l] > arr[start + idx]) idx = l;
        if (r < heapSize && arr[start + r] > arr[start + idx]) idx = r;
        if (idx !== i) { [arr[start + i], arr[start + idx]] = [arr[start + idx], arr[start + i]]; i = idx; } else break;
      }
    }
    for (let i = Math.floor(len / 2) - 1; i >= 0; i--) heapify(i, len);
    for (let endIdx = len - 1; endIdx > 0; endIdx--) {
      swap(start, start + endIdx);
      heapify(0, endIdx);
    }
  }

  function quicksort3(start, end, depth) {
    if (end - start <= 1) return;
    if (depth <= 0) {
      heapSortSub(start, end);
      return;
    }
    const pivot = arr[start + Math.floor((start + end - 1) / 2)];
    let lt = start, i = start, gt = end - 1;
    while (i <= gt) {
      if (arr[i] < pivot) { swap(lt, i); lt++; i++; }
      else if (arr[i] > pivot) { swap(i, gt); gt--; }
      else { i++; }
    }
    quicksort3(start, lt, depth - 1);
    quicksort3(gt + 1, end, depth - 1);
  }

  quicksort3(0, n, depthLimit);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for parallel sorting on large arrays?
- What changes if stability is required while keeping O(n log n) average time?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of in-place merging, a practical skill when space is constrained.

## Key Concepts
- In-place merging with end pointers
- Time O(m+n), space O(1)
- Edge cases: empty arrays, duplicates, negative numbers

## Code Example
```javascript
function mergeInPlace(A, m, B, n) {
  let i = m - 1, j = n - 1, k = m + n - 1;
  while (j >= 0) {
    if (i >= 0 && A[i] > B[j]) {
      A[k--] = A[i--];
    } else {
      A[k--] = B[j--];
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this with random arrays and verify sortedness?
- How would you adapt if A has no extra space and you can only rotate segments?
